<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Cape Passaro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1718">1718</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-quadruple-alliance">War of the Quadruple Alliance</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Cape Passaro was fought on 11 August 1718 off the south-eastern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, where a British fleet under Admiral Sir George Byng destroyed a Spanish fleet supporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s invasion of Sicily. Although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Spain were not yet formally at war, the battle crippled the Spanish navy and helped force Spain to abandon its attempt to recover its former Italian lands.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> of 11 April 1713 gave the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> to Savoy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giulio-alberoni">Giulio Alberoni</a> rebuilt the royal administration and promised Philip V a chance to recover Sicily and Naples. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> proposed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-amadeus-ii">Victor Amadeus II</a> of Savoy exchange Sicily for Sardinia, the emperor's arrest of the Spanish Grand Inquisitor José Molinés at Milan gave Spain its pretext. A Spanish fleet took Sardinia in 1717, and on 18 June 1718 another left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> carrying 36,000 infantry and 8,000 horse under the Marquis of Lede to Sicily.</p><p>Most of the island fell with little resistance, except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a>. Admiral George Byng, who had sailed from Spithead with twenty ships of the line, carried 2,000 German infantry to Messina. Trusting Alberoni's letters that Byng meant no harm, the Spanish commanders José Antonio de Gaztañeta and José Patiño took their fleet south towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Spanish fleet sailed in disorder and by morning lay in three separate groups. Byng sent Captain George Walton with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> and five other ships after the rear under the Marquis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, who drove his ships ashore off Avola and set them on fire. The main British force overhauled Gaztañeta's six ships of the line, taking Santa Rosa, San Carlos and Príncipe de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>. The flagship San Felipe fought for hours against Kent, Superb and Byng's Barfleur and struck only at nightfall, Gaztañeta wounded in the leg. Some ships escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>; Santa Isabel surrendered the next morning. The British lost no more than 500 killed or wounded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Walton reported that he had taken and destroyed all the Spanish ships on the coast. The captured San Felipe later blew up by accident, killing 50 Spanish prisoners and 160 of her British prize crew. The Spanish army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> was cut off. Alberoni banned news of the expedition, issued letters of marque and seized British ships in Spanish ports. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> declared war on 26 December and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on 9 January the following year. Alberoni's answer, a Jacobite expedition to Britain, was scattered by a storm off Cape Finisterre, and about 400 men who landed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> were defeated at Glenshiel.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Cape Passaro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Messina’s revolt of 1674–1678 brought a French fleet, and in 1718 a British fleet destroyed the Spanish navy off <b>Cape Passaro</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Military</a></li></ul></section>
